  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 AM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\n- Increasing clouds today. Showers/storms tonight and Saturday.\n\nIR loop shows abundant high clouds moving over the top of the\nridge and across the state. Clouds will increase today as low\npressure over Iowa strengthens and moves east. Latest models are a\nlittle more bullish on rain chances and amounts across the cwa\ntonight and Saturday. The short wave accompanying the surface low\nis stronger which in turn is helping to draw moisture northward.\nShowers and scattered thunderstorms are expected to develop by \nlate evening across the western cwa and then move eastward. Given\nbulk shear values 20-25kts, severe weather will likely remain\nsouth of the cwa where shear is greater. Locally heavy rainfall\nmay be a bigger threat.\n\nPrecipitable water values increase to over 2 inches after 06z\ntonight suggesting that a few of the heavier showers and/or storms\ncould be prolific rain producers. WPC has painted the cwa in a\nslight risk for excessive rainfall for tonight and Saturday. Thus\nlocalized flooding isn't out of the question. Total qpf from\ntonight through Saturday is in the half inch to inch range basin\nwide; locally higher. \n\nThe low will move east Sunday and rainfall chances will diminish\nslowly throughout the day from northwest to southeast. \n\nClouds and precipitation will hold highs in the mid 70s Saturday\nand Sunday. \n\n- Showers middle of next week\n\nAnother cold front and upper trough will move across the state\nduring the middle of next week generating some showers and storms.\nDespite the deterministic versions of the ECMWF and GFS being\nsomewhat out of phase, the ensembles of both show precipitation\nduring the middle of the week. \n\nHighs will be in the lower to mid 80s through next week.",
